ness` and route `headSha`/`treeAndParent` through the same `run` helper. Or catch timeouts there and return `undefined`, so the page says it could not check.\n\n- **`range-cap`: the notice counts non-merge commits only** (\"of 205 commits\"), while the group describes the cap as \"the newest 200 commits … and the merges since\". The notice wording and the README do not say merges are excluded from the count. This is minor and only a wording issue. **read**\n\n**2. Misgraded necessity:** not assessed, per the review rules.\n\n**3. Removal candidates:** not assessed, per the review rules.\n\n**4. Simplifications**\n\n- In `wipStaleness`, the `try/catch` around `workingTree` could be folded into `workingTree` itself, returning `undefined` on `TooBig` and timeouts. This would also fix the uncovered-call gap in item 1. **read**\n\n**5. Missing risks**\n\n- The wip view now runs a full `git add -A` in a temporary index on every page load, and a slow `git log` or `rev-parse` can turn a wip view into a 422 or a long wait. This is partly listed (cost), but the failure mode of the non-deadlined calls is not.\n- The `.emptyrows` element has no `display` rule, so the `hidden` attribute works. I checked this and found no bug. **read**\n- The j/k skip, the filter, and the rail re-render were checked by reading the code and the tests named in the evidence. I did not re-run those.\n\n**Checked and consistent (read):** `resolveCommits` slices the oldest-first `--no-merges` list with `slice(-newest)`, so it keeps the newest commits as claimed. The merge filter uses the whole-range list. Filter matching is a literal `indexOf` on lower-cased text. The `detached-head` notice wording matches its test.\n\n**Evidence:** I ran only the single scratch experiment in item 1. I did not re-run the orchestrator's test suite.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
