5f3f68f57a783ec","e5f3f68f57a783ec","e77c5ebccaf91712","e7dc8975281a6d18","eb72a8cd0b1c37a4","eb72a8cd0b1c37a4","ecbab55e27a88431","edb3250eabd8e33d","ede6e5b3959470f3","eeef40e85db1fddc","efcfc56d56a94a7b","f0a4377b2a89d571","f0d5516db051e66e","f0e21933dc45b752","f55b417107a6bcea","f5a62a7235193491","f61b01e2474605a6","f98799451cc7effd","fbd172b62ef8506f","fbdf6484f5fb9ca8","fbfa398ed8d485a3","fcd89fb8cd128e5c"],"groups":["1f9cb148ec93def8","4497e5e3b025dd7f","65bb0b14009920d3","a3efe39286ca99b7","13a4dd93ae74d285","68b2135e042d0418","15331c40fc04626e","19cbb2f9e4f3cde0","0327a1973324169f","2d3aca130ba2f97b"],"findings":"**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`desk-docs` (README.md:263), `wip-link`: the server still says it \"never writes anything\", which the new code contradicts. Verified.** `wipStaleness` calls `workingTree()` (src/git.ts:116-138), which runs `git add -A` and `git write-tree` against the real object database. The `wip-link` notes say \"it only writes objects\", so the author knew objects are written. The README sentence was not updated. Scratch repo `/tmp/git-gud-ffcUP1/scratch/wr`: five `/?rev=wip` views with a changed tree took loose objects from 6 to 16, so every view adds objects. They are unreachable and `git gc` will reclaim them, but the claim \"never writes anything\" is false as written. The desk.ts comment \"the server never takes one\" (a snapshot ref) is accurate.\n\n2. **`wip-link` notes: \"the server stays read-only\" is only partly true. Verified** by the object count above. It is read-only with respect to refs and snapshots, not to the object store. The \"Risks\" list also omits this write and the accumulation it causes.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n3. **Object growth on every wip view (verified, see 1).** Each wip page load with a changed working tree adds a blob and tree object per changed path set, with no cleanup. On a repo whose working tree changes often, this is an unbounded write on a page load. The author's risk (\"costs time on a big tree\") covers latency only. Either accept and document it, or check staleness without writing objects (for example by comparing the snapshot's tree with `git diff-files` or the index stat data).\n\n4. **Range capping still lists every commit before slicing (read).** `resolveCommits` runs `rev-list` over the full range, then `desk.ts` keeps the newest 200. A very large range still pays the full `rev-list` cost on each request. This was already true before the change, so it is not a regression, but the notice text (\"Showing the newest 200 of N\") implies the cap limits work, and it does not.\n\n5. **Browser behaviour of the commit filter is not verified by me (read).** The \"in a browser\" test is gated on `skipBrowse`. I did not confirm it ran rather than skipped, so the orchestrator's 409-pass result does not show that the filter's browser behaviour was exercised. The code reads correctly: `includes` on lower-cased short sha, subject and author; no regex or `innerHTML`; the input survives rail re-renders.\n\n**Checked and consistent (verified or read as noted)**\n\n- `detached-head` (read, and the test covers it): when there are no branches, `rev` falls back to `HEAD` with a notice, so `/` no longer 422s. An empty repo with no commits still gets \"master has no commits to show\" (verified in `/tmp/git-gud-ffcUP1/scratch/empty/r`). No regression there.\n- `range-cap` (read): `slice(-200)` on oldest-first `rev-list --reverse` keeps the newest 200. The picker path (`defaultRev`) still shows only the newest commit when a branch range is over 200, as the notes and TODO say.\n- `wip-link` (read): the link and the staleness notice only appear when a snapshot exists, and the staleness check runs only for `rev=wip`, as described. Verified by the scratch run, which showed the \"out of date\" notice.\n- Removed TODOs match the fixes. The README's \"A page shows at most 200 commits; pick a narrower range for more\" is still broadly accurate.\n\n**Simplification (minor, read)**\n\n- `WIP_REF` is looked up twice in `buildDesk`: once for the `wip` fallback and again for `snapshot`. One lookup, reused, would be clearer.\n\n**Necessity grading**: not assessed, per the brief.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
