68f57a783ec","e5f3f68f57a783ec","e77c5ebccaf91712","e7dc8975281a6d18","eb72a8cd0b1c37a4","eb72a8cd0b1c37a4","ecbab55e27a88431","edb3250eabd8e33d","ede6e5b3959470f3","eeef40e85db1fddc","efcfc56d56a94a7b","f0a4377b2a89d571","f0d5516db051e66e","f0e21933dc45b752","f55b417107a6bcea","f5a62a7235193491","f61b01e2474605a6","f98799451cc7effd","fbd172b62ef8506f","fbdf6484f5fb9ca8","fbfa398ed8d485a3","fcd89fb8cd128e5c"],"groups":["1f9cb148ec93def8","4497e5e3b025dd7f","65bb0b14009920d3","a3efe39286ca99b7","13a4dd93ae74d285","68b2135e042d0418","15331c40fc04626e","19cbb2f9e4f3cde0","0327a1973324169f","2d3aca130ba2f97b"],"findings":"**Review of the uncommitted ary changes: wip link and staleness notice, newest-200 for oversized ranges, detached-HEAD fallback, commit filter**\n\n### 1. Opening the wip view now writes git objects, which breaks ary's \"never writes anything\" promise. Major, **verified**\n`src/ary/desk.ts:104` calls `wipStaleness(snapshot)` on every `?rev=wip` request. That function calls `workingTree()` (`src/git.ts:116-138`), which runs `git add -A` and then `git write-tree` against a temporary index. The temporary index goes away, but the blobs and trees are written into the repo's real object database. So just loading or reloading the wip page stores every modified and untracked file in `.git/objects`.\n\n- **What I ran:** In a scratch repo I took a wip snapshot, then created an untracked `new.txt`. Calling `buildDesk({rev:'wip'})` returned 200 with the \"out of date\" notice.\n- **What it showed:** `git count-objects` went from 6 to 8, and `git fsck --unreachable` listed a new unreachable blob and tree.\n\nThe README, unchanged by this diff, says the server \"never writes anything: it doesn't take snapshots or change notes\". The new README sentence repeats it (\"the server never takes one\").\n\nThere is also a cost problem. Every reload of the wip page runs a full `git add -A` and hashes all untracked, non-ignored files, which could be large build output. `git add -A` adds even the files `looksSensitive` excludes (the `.env`-style ones) before removing them from the temporary index, so their blobs are written too. A fix would compare without writing objects, for example with `git diff`/`git status`-style checks against the snapshot tree.\n\n### 2. When a range is cut to the newest 200, the merges are not cut with it. Minor, **read**\nIn `src/ary/desk.ts:98-100`, `shas` is sliced to the newest 200 but `merges` is still the full range's list, and `range` still names the full range.\n\nScenario: `?rev=v1..main` with 5,000 commits and 300 merges. The page shows 200 commits, but \"Merges, not reviewed\" lists all 300, most of them older than anything shown, and the list has no limit. Before this change a range that big was refused outright.\n\n### 3. A range with only merge commits shows \"No commits match.\" with an empty filter box. Minor, **read**\n`src/html/client.js:977-982`: when `railRows` is empty, `shown` stays 0, so `noMatch` is visible even though nothing was typed. That happens when `resolveCommits` returns only merges for a range (`multi` is still true because `data.range` is set). The rail then says no commits match a filter the user never entered.\n\n### 4. The detached-HEAD notice says \"There are no branches\" when remote branches exist. Minor, **read**\n`src/ary/desk.ts:84`: `fallback` only looks at local branches. In a clone with remotes but no local branches, the notice says there are no branches while the Branch picker lists the remote ones.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
