hides (minor, read)\n`src/html/client.js:493-497`. On a range, `stepTo` walks the full `commitOrder()` and ignores `railRows[].node.hidden`.\n- **Scenario:** on `?rev=main..feature`, type `two` in the filter box so only commit \"two\" is listed. Click it, move focus out of the input, then press `j`/`k`.\n- **Outcome:** the pane opens commit \"one\", which the filter hides. The rail then has no visible selected row, and the user is reviewing a commit the filter excluded.\n\n### 2. The \"10 s\" limit applies to each git call, and the file listing has no limit (minor, read)\n`src/git.ts:134-147`. `READ_ONLY_LIMIT_MS` is passed to each of `add`, `rm --cached` and `write-tree`. The `ls-files --others` that counts untracked files, and the `rev-parse --git-path objects` call, still run under the full `GIT_TIMEOUT`.\n- **Scenario:** a working tree where listing untracked files is slow (for example, a huge un-ignored tree), or where `add` and `write-tree` each take close to 10 s.\n- **Outcome:** a wip page can take about 30 s plus the full listing time before \"Could not check\" appears. This contradicts the README's \"gives up … after 10 s\". It could also push the page build past `GIT_GLOSS_ARY_TIMEOUT` and return a 504 instead of the promised notice.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
